it's great to be joined today by Brian  cloths who's a fellow in global and  comparative politics at the London  School of Economics also author of the  book the desperate accomplice How the  West is aiding and abetting the decline  of democracy it's so great to have you  on I think as a sort of general  principle we would consider that at the  world level we've seen more democracy  since the Cold War and you argue in the  book that we've now seen a reversal in  that so I guess the first question is  how do we measure democracy in the world  sure that's a good question so you're  absolutely right we had a massive surge  in democracy through the 1980s 90s and  early 2000 so Latin America democratized  me 80s then we had Eastern Europe and  sub-saharan Africa the 90s after the  Berlin Wall fell and then it started to  switch and this tipping point happened  around 2006 according to the major  indices this doesn't happen always with  you know coos or civil wars or you know  massive authoritarian grabs of power  it's a slippage of what a lot of  political scientists called Democratic  backsliding but basically there's a lot  of indicators that look at the quality  of democracy and the harsh reality is  that for 11 years we've been going in  the wrong direction so authoritarianism  is on the march and democracy is in  retreat and what are some of the I mean  generally we've talked a lot about  authoritarianism certainly in the last  year's a year and a half but what are  some of the more specific indicators if  we were to sort of create some kind of  democracy score or mean or something  like that  what sort of cycle can we look at so  election quality is certainly one of  them how free and fair elections are  there's also press freedom there's other  aspects about how basically civil  society is treated how democratic the  process that the rule of law is in  making laws there's a whole variety of  others there's a few different indices  polity for does one freedom house does  another there's one called varieties of  democracy that's a think-tank in Sweden  so you know there's a variety different  ways of measuring it but the unifying  thread all of these different  measurement  so they're seeing the same trend this  doesn't mean the Democratic sky is  falling so you know more people live in  democracy in human history  now they sleep than any other time  except for you know the peak about a  decade ago so we're still doing  relatively well by historic standards in  a long long trend but we are looking at  a consistent and very troubling decline  and I'm worried that right now with  policy changes that are happening  sweeping around the world that decline  is actually getting worse before it gets  better  are there or is there a place I guess is  the question when we think about  democracy for factors such as income  inequality for example or economic  mobility right the idea of how easily  can one move out of poverty if they are  born into poverty for example are those  factors that have a role to play in  thinking about democracy around the  world yeah so if you if you think about  the actual word democracy comes from two  greek words demos and craftiness and i  think what you're referring to is  usually lumped into the process so the  demos is the people the rule of the  people and the process is the power and  this is where you know democracy itself  is only good insofar as it actually  generates results for people so I say  very clearly in the book and I don't  think this is a terribly controversial  statement but it's one you don't think  about it that often is you'd probably  not rather be born in Bennett in West  Africa even though it's a democratic  state compared to Singapore which is an  authoritarian state because you have to  basically work for 65 years in Benin in  order to receive the same salary you  probably would get in Singapore and at  some point the right to vote is not  worth that much to a lot of people so I  think what's what a lot of people have  lost track of is that democracy can be a  world and basic good no matter what  because it's important to have people  how to say in the government but it also  has to deliver and I think one thing  that's hollowed out the quality of  democracy in the West that has given  rise to populism is a lot of people  living in democratic states have seen  their wages stagnate they haven't been  able to get ahead and they're starting  to turn to alternative models or return  to ways of thinking and I think that's a  mistake even though I think it's also a  wake-up call  we need to reinvigorate the quality of  our democracy hmm as far as populism is  concerned we've talked a lot on the  program about how you can have sort of  similar sounding populist rhetoric from  potential or actual elected officials  but that in terms of governance there's  a pretty big difference between what  we've sort of called progressive  populism versus regressive populism can  you talk a little bit about how the  populist message more broadly cannot  always directly relate to whether we're  going to see an increase or decrease in  democracy or sort of how populist  rhetoric can maybe muddy the waters in  some sense yeah so I mean a lot of the  surge in popular around the world let's  if we look outside the West sort of  stereotypical cases of you know the rise  of populism in France right now or in  the u.s. around the world you typically  have a liberal populism so this is where  you know you have echoes of this in the  West in the sense of Donald Trump's  message of I alone can fix it it's sort  of an illiberal populist message it's  blending authoritarian rhetoric along  with this idea that the little guy needs  somebody else to look out for them I  mean the irony of all this is that very  often the standard bearers of populism  are the ones who take advantage of  inequality to ride to political office  and then make political and economic  inequality worse once they get there and  that's what's happening in the u.s.  right now and I would argue will  probably accelerate under-under for  example Trump's tax plan but you know  the aspect that I think was the really  big missed opportunity is that there is  a genuine possibility for people in the  last the center-left etc to say we  messed up we have basically ignored at  the center of our country the center of  our economy for 30 years we haven't done  enough to blunt the sharp edges of  globalization but we're going to do this  in a way that is about you know bridges  rather than walls it's about trying to  actually create strategies to fix the  problems rather than just simply  demonize people and blame them for them  so you know I think that's the real  missed opportunity that's happened in  the West and that's something that's  very dangerous because the more people  believe that all  Oh tix is bad and that's part of what  populist messages the more that they  turn to figures who are simply  opportunists and I think that's what  we're seeing in a lot of places where  you know scapegoating is not actually a  strategy and when people begin to  realize that robots and automation are  much bigger threat to them than China or  Mexico or immigrants or whatever it is  they need somebody to pick up the pieces  and I'm hoping that you know as the sort  of tide switches back towards the adults  so to speak the people who have skills  and qualifications there needs to be  somebody ready to pick up that mantle  who can speak the populist language but  also have a pragmatic strategy Brian as  far as religion is concerned not in a  sort of abstract way but specifically as  we've seen it applied around the world  since the Cold War can we make a general  assessment about whether religion is  currently a pro or anti-democratic  influence in the world yeah I mean it's  very mixed I would say I think the  religion has been exploited for  authoritarian reasons sometimes but very  often in history remember of some of the  most tyrannical governments are  completely secular and actually used the  tools of the state to dismantle religion  I think you know it's it's somewhat  neutral to in a sense depends on how  it's used and how it's sort of you know  employed in pursuit of a specific  ideology but I would say that you know  there's there's this there is a trend I  mean this has been identified for for a  long time there's a famous article in  political science called Mick world  versus jihad and this idea of are we  going to be this big global force which  has maybe less room for religious local  government for example or are we going  to have sort of these village based  politics tribal politics and feeling  like we can only look out for ourselves  and not for the other and religion  certainly comes into that divide so you  know I think that there's certainly a  place for religion in the modern world  as long as it is not simply used to  divide people and what I think is really  dangerous is when you have an illiberal  populist message and authoritarian  wrapped up in a foreign rhetoric  that then uses religion as a tool or a  weapon so you know I don't think it's  anything that to make me good or bad I  think it just depends on how people  exploit it or choose to work along the  lines of those values that people hold  in thinking about American foreign  policy for a second there are those in  the United States who have more  interventionist leanings and part of  that is based around this idea of  spreading democracy we heard that from  the George W Bush administration for  example is there any way we can assess  whether Western efforts to quote spread  democracy actually have a pro-democratic  influence sort of in the net sure so one  of the chapters of the book is very  explicitly says no more democracy wars  right so first off I think we need to  make really clear that trying to spread  democracy by the barrel of the gun does  not work and it's very often  counterproductive and what's even worse  than a lot of people don't think about  is this tipping point of the retreat of  democracy in 2006 I think is very  largely linked to the Iraq war and the  fact that authoritarian regimes around  the world were able to credibly claim  that any sort of u.s. pressure in  support of human rights or pro-democracy  NGOs on the ground or whatever was just  a Trojan horse to prepare for regime  change and the Iraq war gave them that  credibility because they could point to  it and say look they did it elsewhere so  what ended up happening was all the  sudden these regimes around the world  started purging NGOs for formers etc and  just saying this is the precursor to a  CIA tactic or a US invasion and of  course it wasn't the case but they have  the legitimacy to say that because the  Iraq war the second aspect of this is I  think that there's a very big problem  with how our foreign policy signals  things abroad and this is what I'm most  worried about now I think the u.s. can  actually legitimately spread democracy  effectively if it doesn't right but  what's happening now is sort of a  perfect storm of every bad thing the US  could do and what I mean by that is that  over the weekend you had Trump inviting  Rodrigo Duterte of the Philippines to  the White House right he embraced  President Sisi of Egypt he congratulated  President Erdogan of Turkey on rigging a  referendum that dismantled democracy  within a NATO ally and people around the  world I mean one thing we don't think  about I love it often but I saw you know  in my travels from Madagascar to Belarus  etc was every regime around the world  looks to Washington for cues about what  it can get away with and when you had  those signals coming out of the White  House it is extremely counterproductive  and and it sends a signal that the u.s.  doesn't even care anymore you know one  of the things that I found striking was  I went back to Trump's old tweets and I  found that he is tweeted as of yesterday  he tweeted for the 247th time about  crowds right he's tweeted about human  rights once and the only time he tweeted  about them was to mock Secretary of  State John Kerry for caring about them  so when you have that message you know  every government around the world sees  that and thinks you know if we torture a  few journalists I don't really think  that's going to be a big deal to trump  and they're probably right so you know I  think that it takes a very smart  strategy which is you know I outlined  ten principles for how the US can be  more effective in the book but I think  that we are going down a very dark path  where the president is actually  cheerleading these terrible governments  as opposed to acting like a referee that  can push them to follow the rules well  the book is incredibly timely again it's  called the despot sakam plus how the  West is aiding and abetting the decline  of democracy we've been speaking with  the book's author Brian cloths Brian  thanks so much for talking to me today  it's my pleasure thanks for having on  the show

